<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Sybaris</h1><p class="st-meta">720 BCE – 510 BCE (approx.) · capital Sybaris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Achaean Greek city in southern Italy so rich that its name became a byword for luxury, destroyed by its neighbour Croton in 510 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Achaean colonists from the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, led by Is of Helike, founded Sybaris around 720 BCE on the plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crati">Crathis</a> and Sybaris rivers, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>. The fertile plain and routes across the Calabrian isthmus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> gave it rapid growth.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sybaris became one of the largest Greek cities in the West. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says it ruled four native peoples and twenty-five subject towns and could field an enormous army. It founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/poseidonia">Poseidonia</a> and other colonies, and its traders worked closely with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>. Later Greek writers delighted in tales of Sybarite excess, which gave English the word 'sybarite'. Around 510 BCE a popular leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telys">Telys</a>, seized power and expelled the wealthy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/croton">Croton</a> sheltered the Sybarite exiles, war followed. In 510 BCE Croton's army, led by the athlete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milo-of-croton">Milo</a>, defeated Sybaris, and the victors are said to have diverted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crati">Crathis</a> over the ruins. Attempts at refoundation led to the Panhellenic colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thurii">Thurii</a> in 444 BCE nearby.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sybaris.jpg" alt="Sybaris" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Mboesch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>720 BCE – 510 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sybaris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Telys</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Achaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-615&amp;focus=sybaris" aria-label="Sybaris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5087 756 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sybaris lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5407 1012L5406 1011M5407 1012L5368 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5406 1011h0M5368 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5407 1012h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5407 1012h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-615&amp;focus=sybaris">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
